The only difference between Figure 4e and Figure 4d is $K\times miscost > min(M_{0}, M_{1})$ . It makes $(M_{1}+M_{2}-K\times miscost)< max(M_{0}, M_{1})$ , so we cannot use the method of Figure 4d in this case. We need to extend $A_{0}$ downward and $A_{1}$ upward simultaneously. The extending methods are the same with those of Figure 4b and Figure 4c. Suppose that the last scores of $A_{0}$ and $A_{1}$ are $M_{0}^{\prime}$ and $M_{1}^{\prime}$ respectively, the final result A is the local alignment, whose score is max $(M_{0}^{\prime}, M_{1}^{\prime})$ .

Figure 4f shows $j_{0}<|T|, j_{1}>1$ and $j_{0}>j_{1}$ . Because of the existence of the overlap between $A_{0}$ and $A_{1}$ , we cannot combine $A_{0}$ and $A_{1}$ together using the method of Figure 4d. In this case, $A_{0}$ and $A_{1}$ will be extended in a way similar to that of Figure 4e.

Suppose a parallel system has p (p>2) processors. The C&E method on the system is similar to the method on two processors. The method can be described as follows:

1 Arrange all the alignments $A_{i}$ （ $i=0, 1\dots , p-1$ ） on descending order, according to their scores;

2 Let $A_{\alpha}$ and $A_{\beta}$ , which locate in processor $P_{\alpha}$ and $P_{\beta}$ respectively, be the two maximal alignments;

3 If $A_{\alpha}$ adjoins $A_{\beta}$ , apply the C&E method in Figure 4 to manipulate $A_{\alpha}$ and $A_{\beta}$ . The $A_{\beta}$ is deleted from the descending order and the result is denoted as $A_{\alpha}$ ;
